head_commit.message:
[XLA] Factor LiteralBase::Convert type dispatch through canonical intermediate types.

Factor the quadratic 32x31 pairwise primitive-type conversion matrix in `LiteralBase::Convert` (`xla/literal.cc`) through the four lossless canonical scalar representations (`int64_t` for signed integers `S1..S64`, `uint64_t` for `PRED` and unsigned integers `U1..U64`, `float` for narrow and 32-bit floats `F4..BF16` and `F32`, and `double` for `F64`), plus direct `C64 <-> C128` complex conversion. Previously, `ConvertSwitch` and `ConvertIfDestTypeMatches<kSrcType>` instantiated `ConvertBetweenNativeTypes<NativeSrcT, NativeDestT>` across all 992 `(SrcType, DstType)` pairs, duplicating software float8/float6/float4 and sub-byte integer decoding/encoding loops across every source-destination combination (`478 KB` of `.text` in `literal.pic.o`).

```
// Before: O(N^2) pairwise conversion loops (32 x 31 = 992 instantiations)
template <PrimitiveType kSrcType>
bool ConvertIfDestTypeMatches(...) {
  switch (dst_type) {
    case S1: return ConvertBetweenNativeTypes<NativeSrcT, int1>(...);
    ...
    case C128: return ConvertBetweenNativeTypes<NativeSrcT, complex128>(...);
  }
}

// After: O(N) canonical widening + ConvertFromCanonical<CanonicalT>
//   S1..S64        -> int64_t  -> DstType
//   PRED, U1..U64  -> uint64_t -> DstType
//   F4..BF16, F32  -> float    -> DstType
//   F64            -> double   -> DstType
```

All 13 narrow floating-point formats (`F4E2M1FN`, `F6E2M3FN`, `F6E3M2FN`, `F8E3M4`, `F8E4M3`, `F8E4M3FN`, `F8E4M3B11FNUZ`, `F8E4M3FNUZ`, `F8E5M2`, `F8E5M2FNUZ`, `F8E8M0FNU`, `F16`, and `BF16`) have strictly fewer exponent and mantissa bits than IEEE-754 `float`, so every finite, zero, subnormal, infinite, and NaN value is represented losslessly in `float`.

**Runtime and Memory Impact**:
- When the source type is already canonical (`S64`, `U64`, `F32`, `F64`) or the destination matches the canonical type, conversion executes in a single pass directly into the destination buffer with zero intermediate copy.
- For non-canonical pairs, conversion processes 512-element (`2-4 KB`, L1-cache-resident) stack blocks, using zero heap allocations. For narrow-float-to-integer conversions, widening to `float` once per element avoids decoding each software `ml_dtypes` value three times per element (for NaN check, bounds clamping, and integer cast) and allows the clamp-and-cast loop over `float` to vectorize.

